---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const raid = assertion('the-night-the-jail-was-taken')

const succession = assertion('the-office-passed-from-father-to-son')

const jail = assertion('three-times-a-mob-came-to-the-jail')

const jailSpec = bar(
  jail.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  jail.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Black voters in Lima', labelValues: true },
)

const jailCrowds = [
  ['April 1872', "Andrew Brentlinger's execution", 'Fifty armed men guard the jail against a threatened mob. No one is hurt.'],
  ['30 Aug 1916', 'A mob demands a Black prisoner', 'Sheriff Sherman Eley hides Charles Daniels and refuses to say where. The mob puts a rope round his neck; he is hospitalised and later given a medal.'],
  ['12 Oct 1933', 'The jail raid', 'Sheriff Jess Sarber is shot dead in his own jail.'],
]
---

<Article slug="three-times-a-crowd-came-to-the-jail">
  <AssertionCard assertion={jail} />
  <p>
    This site has held the raid of October 1933 since its early phases — the night Sheriff Jess
    Sarber was shot dead in the Allen County jail. It turns out to be the third such night, not
    the first, and the 1921 county history had both of the others on facing pages.
  </p>
  <DataTable
    title="Sixty-one years, three crowds"
    caption="The 1921 history states the sheriff's duty in the same paragraph as its roster of holders: he 'is provided with a domicile in connection with the bastile, and it becomes his duty to prevent lynchings, riots and all violent disorders.'"
    columns={['When', 'What', 'What happened']}
    rows={jailCrowds}
  />
  <p>
    The book cannot give a day for the 1872 hanging — it prints "Friday, April 7 or 15, 1872 —
    there being conflicting reports about it". Neither date was a Friday. The 7th was a Sunday and
    the 15th a Monday, and the Fridays that April were the 5th, 12th, 19th and 26th. Governor
    Rutherford B. Hayes had granted the condemned man a hundred days' reprieve from a sentence
    date of 20 January, which lands on 29 April, later than either.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same volume carries the only description this site holds of the county's Black population
    before the census tables of the present century: about 2,500 people in 1921, nearly all of
    them in Lima, with four congregations and five fraternal orders. Its two voter counts are four
    years apart and the second is the first to include women.
  </p>
  <ChartFigure
    id="lima-black-voters"
    title="Black voters in Lima, as the 1921 county history reports them"
    caption="The book gives one cause — migration into the county — for a change that also spans the Nineteenth Amendment, and does not separate the two. Both figures are the book's estimates, not counts."
    columns={['Year', 'Voters']}
    rows={jail.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="lima-black-voters" set:html={JSON.stringify(jailSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    It is a source that has to be read against itself. On one page: "While most of the negroes
    live in certain localities, there is no segregation." On the next: "It is said the negroes do
    not live north of the Pennsylvania railway tracks in Lima," and "once in a while the color
    line is drawn, and the negroes remain south of the railway tracks for safety." This site
    records the contradiction and does not resolve it. The same chapter says the county's Jewish
    community began with Levi Jacobs about 1850 and "never located in any other town in Allen
    County" — a dozen families in 1878, about a hundred and some 350 people by 1921, seven years
    after they built Temple Beth Israel.
  </p>

  <h2>What it changed about this county's government</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={succession} />
  <p>
    The sheriff's roster on this site has shown the same two lines since the corpus first read
    it: Jess L. Sarber, 1931–1933, and then Donald F. Sarber, 1933–1935. A two-year term ending
    mid-decade, and the same surname straight after it. The roster gives no reason for either,
    and for eleven phases this corpus declined to supply one, because a record saying
    <em>died in office</em> and a guess saying <em>died in office</em> are indistinguishable once
    written down.
  </p>
  <p>
    It took a newspaper to settle it, and not the one anyone would have looked for. No Lima paper
    is digitized in the national archive — the county seat's press is simply absent — so the
    answer came from a wire story about a trial, reprinted in a Washington evening paper five
    months after the fact, which calls the murdered man "the elder Sarber, who preceded his son
    as sheriff".
  </p>
  <p>
    The other irregular line in the roster is still unexplained. James K. Everett begins and ends
    in 2017, the only holder in thirty-nine whose term does not cross a year, and the obvious
    reading — an appointment to fill a vacancy — is still only the obvious reading.
  </p>
</Article>
